1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Why the kernel is slow for packets — AxioByte Systems

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A 10-gigabit link can fire nearly 15 million packets every second. Can the kernel handle each one?

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,500
Follow one packet up the normal path, through the kernel.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:16,000
First, the NIC raises an interrupt. The CPU drops what it's doing to service it.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Then the packet is copied into a kernel buffer, and the whole TCP/IP stack runs on it.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,500
Then a context switch hands control from the kernel to your app in user space.

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,500
And it's copied a second time, into your buffer. Watch the latency meter climb.

8
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,000
Every single packet pays that tax — so the kernel tops out around half a million packets a second.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
DPDK's answer: bypass the kernel entirely.

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,500
The NIC writes packets straight into your process's memory — hugepage-backed user-space mempool.

11
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:48,500
A poll-mode driver busy-polls the card. No interrupts, no copies, no syscalls.

12
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:53,000
Instead of blocking on recv, you just poll a burst of packets. The latency stays flat.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
The trade-off: a whole core spins at 100%, and you give up the kernel's stack — you build what you need.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,500
But it takes you from half a million to tens of millions of packets per second, per core.

15
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,000
Skip the kernel, reach line rate.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,500
AxioByte Systems — high-performance systems from first principles. Follow for the series.
